findings.recommendation:
Either accept and document, or close the seam. Accepting is defensible — the round 2 test suite already pins `moveGroup(badOrder, ...) === []` explicitly, so this is a deliberate, tested contract rather than an accident; in that case just correct the JSDoc to say what actually happens, e.g. "Если движение невозможно — возвращается копия исходного порядка; если `order` вообще не массив — пустой массив." If you prefer the invariant to hold unconditionally, hoist the shape check into moveGroup and make the two layers agree with the sanitizer instead:

  export function moveGroup(order, groupId, direction, hiddenGroups) {
    if (!Array.isArray(order)) return [];  // or [...GROUP_IDS], matching sanitizeGroupOrder
    ...
  }

and state in the doc comment that the agreement invariant is scoped to array inputs. Either way the guidance for Task 08 is the same and worth writing down: call canMoveGroup first and only assign moveGroup's result when it returns true.

Unchanged from round 1, and belongs to Task 03. Mirror the existing sanitizer for the visibility set and apply it in the same unconditional block:

  export function sanitizeHiddenGroups(value: unknown): GroupId[] {
    if (!Array.isArray(value)) return [];
    return [...new Set(value.filter((id): id is GroupId => GROUP_IDS.includes(id as GroupId)))];
  }

called next to `board.groupOrder = sanitizeGroupOrder(board.groupOrder)`. Note the deliberate asymmetry with sanitizeGroupOrder's non-array fallback: an unreadable order must be rebuilt to all six ids, whereas an unreadable visibility set should fail open to "nothing hidden" rather than invent hidden groups.
